Pattern 5: Expression Handling (Parsing)
Evaluating math equations or string formatting.

1. The Concept
You process a string character by character.

Numbers/letters go onto the stack.

When you hit an operator (+, -, *) or a closing bracket (), ]), you pop from the stack, perform an operation, and push the result back onto the stack.

2. Java Blueprint (Basic Calculator II - no brackets)
Java:

public int calculate(String s) {
    Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
    int currentNumber = 0;
    char operation = '+'; // Default first operation
    
    for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
        char c = s.charAt(i);
        
        // Build the number if it's multiple digits (e.g., "123")
        if (Character.isDigit(c)) {
            currentNumber = (currentNumber * 10) + (c - '0');
        }
        
        // If we hit an operator OR the end of the string
        if (!Character.isDigit(c) && !Character.isWhitespace(c) || i == s.length() - 1) {
            if (operation == '+') stack.push(currentNumber);
            else if (operation == '-') stack.push(-currentNumber);
            else if (operation == '*') stack.push(stack.pop() * currentNumber);
            else if (operation == '/') stack.push(stack.pop() / currentNumber);
            
            operation = c;
            currentNumber = 0; // Reset for the next number
        }
    }
    
    // Sum everything left in the stack
    int result = 0;
    while (!stack.isEmpty()) result += stack.pop();
    return result;
}

3. Essential Interview Questions
Easy: Valid Parentheses (LeetCode 20) - Push opening brackets. If closing bracket, pop and check if they match.

Medium: Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation (LeetCode 150)

Medium: Decode String (LeetCode 394) - Use two stacks: one for numbers (repeat counts) and one for building the strings.
